            "summary": "Blood as a fluid connective tissue, physical properties, plasma composition (90-92% water, 6-8% proteins), albumin, globulins, fibrinogen, and definition of serum.",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<p><strong>Blood</strong> is a specialized, mobile fluid connective tissue originating from the embryonic mesoderm. In an adult human, blood constitutes roughly <strong>7% to 8% of total body weight</strong> (an average volume of <strong>5.0 to 5.5 liters</strong>). It has a slightly alkaline pH of <strong>7.35 to 7.45</strong>, a specific gravity of 1.050 to 1.060, and an osmolality of ~290–300 mOsm/kg.</p>\n<p><strong>Blood Composition:</strong>\nWhen anticoagulated blood is centrifuged, it separates into two primary phases:\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Plasma (~55% of blood volume):</strong> A straw-colored, viscous extracellular fluid matrix.</li>\n<li><strong>Formed Elements (~45% of blood volume):</strong> Cellular components comprising Erythrocytes (RBCs), Leukocytes (WBCs), and Thrombocytes (Platelets).</li>\n</ol>\n</p>\n<p><strong>Plasma Components:</strong>\nPlasma comprises:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Water (90–92%):</strong> Acts as the universal solvent and medium of thermal distribution throughout the body.</li>\n<li><strong>Plasma Proteins (6–8% / 60–80 g/L):</strong> Synthesized primarily by the liver (except immunoglobulins):\n  <ul>\n    <li><strong>Albumin (~35–50 g/L, 55–60% of total plasma protein):</strong> Smallest but most abundant plasma protein. Responsible for generating approximately <strong>75% to 80% of the Colloid Osmotic Pressure (Oncotic Pressure, ~25 mmHg)</strong> of plasma, which draws interstitial fluid back into venous capillaries. Hypoalbuminemia (from liver cirrhosis, malnutrition, or nephrotic syndrome) collapses oncotic pressure, producing generalized edema (anasarca). Also serves as a non-specific carrier for hydrophobic drugs, fatty acids, bilirubin, and $\\text{Ca}^{2+}$.</li>\n    <li><strong>Globulins (~20–35 g/L, 35–40% of plasma protein):</strong> Fractionated electrophoretically into:\n      <ul>\n        <li><em>$\\alpha$-Globulins & $\\beta$-Globulins:</em> Transport proteins synthesized by the liver (e.g., Transferrin transports iron; Ceruloplasmin transports copper; Haptoglobin binds free hemoglobin).</li>\n        <li><em>$\\gamma$-Globulins (Immunoglobulins / Antibodies):</em> Synthesized by plasma cells (derived from activated B-lymphocytes) for humoral immunity ($\\text{IgG, IgM, IgA, IgD, IgE}$).</li>\n      </ul>\n    </li>\n    <li><strong>Fibrinogen (~2–4 g/L, ~4% of plasma protein):</strong> Large, soluble fibrous glycoprotein synthesized by hepatocytes; converted into insoluble fibrin strands during blood coagulation (Factor I).</li>\n    <li><strong>Prothrombin (Factor II):</strong> Zymogen precursor to the serine protease thrombin.</li>\n  </ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Inorganic Electrolytes (0.9%):</strong> $\\text{Na}^+$ (predominant extracellular cation), $\\text{K}^+$, $\\text{Ca}^{2+}$, $\\text{Mg}^{2+}$, $\\text{Cl}^-$, $\\text{HCO}_3^-$ (predominant buffer), $\\text{HPO}_4^{2-}$.</li>\n<li><strong>Nutrients & Metabolites:</strong> Glucose ($70 - 100\\text{ mg/dL}$ fasting), amino acids, cholesterol, triglycerides.</li>\n<li><strong>Nitrogenous Waste Products:</strong> Urea ($15 - 40\\text{ mg/dL}$), creatinine ($0.6 - 1.2\\text{ mg/dL}$), uric acid, bilirubin.</li>\n<li><strong>Regulatory Substances:</strong> Hormones, systemic enzymes, vitamins, and dissolved respiratory gases ($\\text{O}_2, \\text{CO}_2, \\text{N}_2$).</li>\n</ul>\n</p>\n<p><strong>Definition of Serum:</strong>\n$$\\mathbf{Serum} = \\mathbf{Plasma} - \\mathbf{Clotting\\ Factors\\ (Fibrinogen\\ \\&\\ Prothrombin)}$$\nWhen whole blood is allowed to clot spontaneously in a test tube, the yellowish supernatant fluid that separates from the retracted clot is <strong>Serum</strong>. Serum lacks fibrinogen and other consumable clotting factors, meaning it cannot clot.</p>\n",
